   1  Architecture · operators-mcp / coding_pool
   2  # How the harness is built
   3  The harness is one Go server that holds the catalog (agents, skills, MCP servers), the work (projects, zones, tickets, documents) and the live agent sessions that act on it. It is built as a hexagonal (ports & adapters) architecture, with its use cases split into DDD bounded contexts and wired at a single composition root. Three clients sit on the same ports: the web UI, the terminal client and the agents themselves through MCP.
   4  1 · System overview 2 · Layers & dependency rule 3 · Bounded contexts 4 · Patterns in use 5 · Folder structure 6 · The web client 7 · A request, end to end 8 · Where new code goes
   5  LanguageGo 1.25 · module operators-mcp
   6  Wiringuber-go/fx modules in internal/app
   7  StorageSQLite via GORM, plus agents/ on disk
   8  InboundHTTP /api + SSE :8080, MCP :8081
   9  Webtempl BFF + Alpine.js ES modules
  10  ## 01System overview
  11  Who talks to the server, and what the server talks to.
  12  Browser templ pages + Alpine.js web/ · /api · SSE tui-client Bubble Tea terminal UI cmd/tui-client · HTTP Agent sessions claude CLI, spawned by us MCP · /mcp/task/… Harness server cmd/server → internal/app (fx) Inbound adaptershttpapi · web · mcp · mcpsession Application servicesone package per context Portsdriving + driven interfaces Domainentities · events · errors Outbound adapterssqlite · gitcli · termhost · … SQLiteGORM · DB_PATH agents/ on diskagents.json · skills/ gitworktrees · history PTY + shellterminals on this machine claude CLI · LLMs~/.claude · Genkit the server spawns the sessions that call it back
  13  There is one process with several entry points. The server (cmd/server) binds the application services to its inbound adapters. The tui-client (cmd/tui-client) binds HTTP adapters to the same driving port interfaces, so a screen holding a ports.TicketBoard cannot tell whether it is in-process or across the network. Agent sessions are spawned by the server and come back through a session-scoped MCP endpoint, so their tools always act on their own task.
  14  ## 02Layers & the dependency rule
  15  Dependencies point inward. Only the composition root sees every layer.
  16  internal/app — composition root (fx) constructs adapters, binds them to ports, hands them to services internal/adapter — in / out HTTP, templ pages, MCP · SQLite, git, PTY, shell, event bus, claude CLI internal/application — use cases per bounded context projects · architecture · agents · capabilities · settings · planning · orchestration … internal/ports — interfaces driving (what services offer) · driven (what services need) internal/domain entities & value types · domain events StructuredError with stable codes imports nothing from this repo
  17  domain ports application adapters composition root
  18  domain → nothingPure Go types: Project, Zone, Agent, Ticket, Session, Artifact… plus events and errors.
  19  ports → domainInterfaces only, expressed in domain types. No implementation lives here except contract test suites.
  20  application → ports, domainServices depend on driven ports, never on SQLite, git or HTTP packages. Each asserts the driving ports it satisfies at compile time.
  21  adapters → ports, domainInbound adapters call driving ports; outbound adapters implement driven ports.
  22  No context imports anotherContexts read each other through read ports and react to each other through domain events on the bus.
  23  Only internal/app constructsConcrete adapters are chosen once, in fx modules, and bound with fx.As(port).
  24  ### Driving vs. driven ports
  25  Kind | Direction | Examples in internal/ports | Implemented by
  26  driving | outside → in | Orchestration (= SessionLauncher, SessionControl, SessionReader, SessionStream, SessionFeed, …), Planning, TicketReader, TicketBoard | application services in the server; adapter/out/httpclient in tui-client
  27  driven | in → outside | TicketRepository, DocumentRepository, ProjectRepository, EventPublisher/EventSubscriber, Shell, PTY, Agent, TerminalHost, ToolRegistry | adapter/out/*
  28  ## 03Bounded contexts
  29  Each context is one package under internal/application, with its own service and the language it owns.
  30  Catalog: wired together in internal/app/catalog over one event bus
  31  #### Projects
  32  application/projects
  33  Projects and the git repositories they are made of, env files, run commands.
  34  emits ProjectDeleted
  35  #### Architecture
  36  application/architecture
  37  How a project's code is carved into zones and bounded contexts; its file tree.
  38  reacts ProjectDeleted, AgentDeleted, PromptDeleted
  39  #### Agents
  40  application/agents
  41  Agent templates and the prompts they are built from. References capabilities by id through CapabilityReader.
  42  emits AgentDeleted, PromptDeleted
  43  reacts SkillDeleted, MCPServerDeleted
  44  #### Capabilities
  45  application/capabilities
  46  What an agent can be equipped with: skills, MCP servers, user-defined tools. Core use cases live in harnesskit.
  47  emits SkillDeleted, MCPServerDeleted
  48  reacts SettingsChanged
  49  #### Settings
  50  application/settings
  51  Global key/value configuration. Validates and stores; what a change means belongs to the context that owns it.
  52  emits SettingsChanged
  53  Work and runtime: separate fx modules
  54  #### Planning
  55  application/planning
  56  Tickets (tasks on the board) and documents, plus their many-to-many link and document scope (task or project).
  57  announces ticket changes on the project feed
  58  #### Orchestration
  59  application/orchestration
  60  Agent sessions: headless (stream-json) and interactive (terminal). Event hub, project feed, questions and approvals, session modes.
  61  #### Execution
  62  application/execution
  63  Runs Genkit flows for a zone's agent with scoped tools and context.
  64  #### Workspaces
  65  application/workspaces
  66  Isolated git worktrees materialised on disk and tracked in the database; repository history.
  67  #### Apps
  68  application/apps
  69  Runs a repository's application from a session's worktree in a server terminal, keeping its output.
  70  #### Artifacts
  71  application/artifacts
  72  What a session published to the Design tab: records it, serves the bytes, announces each publish. Nothing is scanned.
  73  #### Tooling
  74  application/tooling
  75  The registry of built-in tools (filesystem, search/replace, repo map, linter, project/document/context tools) exposed over MCP.
  76  ### Context map
  77  Settings Capabilities Agents Architecture Projects SettingsChanged Skill/MCPServer Deleted Agent/PromptDeleted ProjectDeleted Agents reads Capabilities via CapabilityReader (read port) Events go over adapter/out/eventbus: synchronous, in subscription order, errors joined, so a use case's side effects are done when it returns.
  78  ## 04Patterns in use
  79  What each pattern looks like in this codebase, and where to find it.
  80  Pattern | How it shows up here | Where
  81  Hexagonal / Ports & Adapters | Services see only interfaces; adapters plug in at the edges. The TUI swaps in-process services for HTTP adapters behind the same ports. | internal/ports, internal/adapter
  82  Bounded contexts (DDD) | One package per context, each with its own service and vocabulary. No context imports another. | internal/application/*
  83  Ubiquitous language | Projects, zones, agents, skills, tickets, sessions, artifacts: the same words in the API, CLI, UI copy and code. | PRODUCT.md, internal/domain
  84  Domain events | Small value types with EventName(): project.deleted, skill.deleted, settings.changed… used only for cross-context side effects. | domain/events.go, adapter/out/eventbus
  85  Repository | One driven port per aggregate root (TicketRepository, DocumentRepository…), backed by SQLite, with in-memory fakes for tests. | ports/*.go, adapter/out/persistence/{sqlite,memory}
  86  Application service | A Service struct per context holds the use cases, validates, persists through repositories and announces changes. | application/*/service.go
  87  Read ports (anti-corruption) | A context that needs another's data asks a narrow reader interface (CapabilityReader, TicketReader) instead of its repository. | internal/ports
  88  Composition root + DI | uber-go/fx modules (PersistenceModule, CatalogModule, PlanningModule, ExecutionModule, ServerModule…) build the graph once. | internal/app
  89  Backend for Frontend | templ renders the first paint from driving ports in process; the browser takes over and writes through /api. | adapter/in/web, web/
  90  Contract tests | The same suite runs against the service and against its HTTP adapter over the real router; disagreement means the wire is wrong. | ports/portstest, ports/runtimetest, web/src/modules/*/testing
  91  Structured errors | Every failure carries a stable code (SESSION_NOT_FOUND, DOCUMENT_NOT_HTML…) that surfaces unchanged in API, MCP and UI. | domain/errors.go (harnesskit errs)
  92  Trade-off worth knowing. The domain is thin: internal/domain holds data structures, events and errors, and most invariants (valid ticket status, same project on link, ordering of announcements) are enforced in the application services. That keeps the layers simple, but a rule added in one service is not automatically enforced in another. When an invariant grows, move it onto the domain type.
  93  ## 05Folder structure
  94  Click a folder to expand it. Each line shows the layer and what the folder is responsible for.
  95  Expand allCollapse all
  96  cmd/entryOne main per binary. Parse flags, build the fx graph, run.
  97  server/The harness server: HTTP API + web UI on :8080, MCP on :8081.
  98  tui-client/Terminal client; binds HTTP adapters to the driving ports.
  99  mcp/Standalone MCP server binary.
 100  webbuild/Bundles web/, runs templ generate, regenerates web/test/all.js.
 101  designtokens/Generates CSS tokens from DESIGN.md.
 102  crap/CRAP (complexity × coverage) analyzer for Go/JS/TS/Python.
 103  postman-mcp/Auxiliary MCP server.
 104  internal/All application code. Not importable from outside the module.
 105  domain/domainEntities and value types (Project, Repository, Zone, BoundedContext, Agent, Prompt, Skill, MCPServer, Tool, Ticket, Document, Task, Session, Artifact, Workspace, Settings…), domain events, StructuredError. Imports nothing internal.
 106  events.goCross-context events: ProjectDeleted, AgentDeleted, SkillDeleted, SettingsChanged…
 107  errors.goCoded errors shared by every layer.
 108  ports/portsInterfaces, grouped by context: driving ports the services offer, driven ports they need (repositories, events, terminals, shell, PTY, agents, worktrees).
 109  portstest/Contract suites for network-crossing driving ports (service vs. HTTP adapter).
 110  runtimetest/Contract suites for Shell/PTY; Script agent to test without claude.
 111  application/applicationUse cases, one package per bounded context. Depends on ports and domain only.
 112  projects/Projects, repositories, env files, run commands.
 113  architecture/Zones, bounded contexts, file tree.
 114  agents/Agent templates and prompts.
 115  capabilities/Skills, MCP servers, user tools.
 116  settings/Global key/value configuration.
 117  planning/Tickets, documents and their links.
 118  orchestration/Agent sessions: launch, control, stream, project feed, questions, interactive terminals, task prompts.
 119  modes/Session modes (e.g. design-artifacts, task-architecture) with their prompts and skills.
 120  execution/Genkit flows with scoped tools and context.
 121  tooling/Built-in tool registry exposed over MCP; session-scoped task tools.
 122  workspaces/Git worktrees on disk; repository history.
 123  apps/Running a repository's app from a session worktree.
 124  artifacts/Published artifacts: record, serve, announce, snapshot.
 125  adapter/in/inboundDriving adapters: translate a protocol into calls on driving ports. No business rules.
 126  httpapi/REST /api router, handlers, SSE event streams, auth, CORS, error mapping.
 127  web/Backend for Frontend: templ pages rendered in process from driving ports.
 128  shell/Shared frame: head, top bar, task rail, stream bar.
 129  home/ projects/ tasks/ sessions/ history/One package per page family.
 130  components/templ side of the CUBE CSS blocks.
 131  static/Built assets served with the pages.
 132  mcp/Global MCP server: tool DTOs and handlers over the tool registry.
 133  mcpsession/Per-session MCP surface; session id travels in the path, so tools act on that session's task.
 134  ui/Embedded SPA / dev proxy for the legacy designer UI.
 135  adapter/out/outboundDriven adapters: implement driven ports against real infrastructure.
 136  persistence/Repository implementations.
 137  sqlite/GORM models, mappers and repositories; DB open and migrations.
 138  memory/In-memory repositories for tests.
 139  configrepo/Reads agents, skills and MCP servers live from agents/, merged with the DB. Never writes.
 140  eventbus/Synchronous in-process domain event dispatcher.
 141  gitcli/Shells out to git: worktrees, history, env file discovery.
 142  agents/ports.Agent implementations and claude home access.
 143  claudecli/AgentSpec → interactive claude command line.
 144  command/Plain command in a login shell (e.g. make air).
 145  claudehome/Reads transcripts and config from ~/.claude.
 146  claudetext/Text processing through claude.
 147  termhost/TerminalHost: Agent + Shell + PTY, with a VT emulator clients attach to.
 148  shell/Direct and login-shell command runners.
 149  ptyunix/Unix pseudo-terminal (creack/pty).
 150  filesystem/File tree and path matching.
 151  httpclient/Driving ports over the HTTP API, used by tui-client.
 152  app/rootComposition root: fx modules, server lifecycle, config.
 153  modules.goPersistence, Catalog, Planning, Execution, Tooling, AgentRuntime, Workspaces modules.
 154  server.goMounts /api, MCP, session MCP, web pages; starts/stops listeners.
 155  catalog/Plain constructor wiring the five catalog contexts over one bus (shared by fx and tests).
 156  runtime/fx module for the runtime stack (Agent, Shell, PTY, terminal host).
 157  cliflags/Flags and env fallbacks shared by server and TUI.
 158  infra/infraGenkit (LLM) initialisation.
 159  tuiclient/clientBubble Tea terminal client; sees only driving ports.
 160  wire/fx composition binding HTTP adapters to ports.
 161  app/ nav/Root model, screen stack, screen contract.
 162  screens/projects, tasks, task.
 163  panes/ term/Agent panes that outlive screens; terminal rendering.
 164  live/Follows the project feed, reconnects with backoff.
 165  ui/ tuitest/Shared styles; in-memory fake server.
 166  web/clientBrowser half of the web client: plain ES modules on Alpine.js (CSP build). See section 06.
 167  src/main.jsComposition root: the only place real infrastructure is constructed.
 168  src/shared/Kernel: errors, feed status, api, SSE feed, storage, clock, seed, format.
 169  src/modules/projects, tasks, sessions, runs; each split into domain / infrastructure / presentation / testing.
 170  dev/ test/ testdata/ vendor/In-memory dev pages, browser tests, fixtures shared with Go, pinned Alpine.
 171  design-system/CUBE CSS: global, compositions, utilities, blocks. Tokens come from DESIGN.md.
 172  agents/Live catalog on disk: agents.json, agent folders, skills/. Read by configrepo.
 173  tests/Cross-package suites that run against the wired server.
 174  unit/ contract/ integration/Blueprint matching, tool contracts, MCP + UI embedding.
 175  testhelper/In-process MCP server for tests.
 176  PRODUCT.md · DESIGN.md · MakefileProduct intent, design tokens, build/run targets (make lists them).
 177  ## 06The web client
 178  The same layering repeats in the browser, one module per feature.
 179  web/src/modules/<module>/
 180    domain/          rules + ports.js — the contracts infrastructure fulfils
 181    infrastructure/  gateways over /api and SSE, DTO mappers, memory gateway
 182    presentation/    components, views, register.js (Alpine.data)
 183    testing/         fixtures, stub API, the port's contract suite
 184  SSR first paintThe Go BFF (adapter/in/web) renders the page and embeds a seed; the module hydrates from it.
 185  Writes go to /apiPages only render; mutations travel from the browser to the REST API, and changes come back over SSE.
 186  Layer rulesdomain imports nothing outward; presentation never imports infrastructure; modules never import each other.
 187  One constructorOnly main.js and dev/main.js construct infrastructure.
 188  ## 07A request, end to end
 189  Moving a ticket to review from an agent session, through every layer.
 190  Agent calls an MCP toolThe session's claude CLI calls update_task_status on /mcp/task/<session>/… (adapter/in/mcpsession).
 191  Tool resolves the session's taskA session-scoped tool in application/tooling looks up the session through SessionRepository and finds its ticket.
 192  Use case runsplanning.Service validates the status (domain TicketStatus) and checks the ticket exists, returning a coded error if not.
 193  Persist through a driven portTicketRepository.Update, implemented by adapter/out/persistence/sqlite.
 194  AnnounceUnder ticketMu, the service hands the change to TicketAnnouncer; orchestration's feed puts a ProjectChange on the project stream.
 195  Clients updatehttpapi streams it over SSE; the browser's tasks module and tui-client's live package apply it. No refresh.
 196  ## 08Where new code goes
 197  I need to… | Put it in
 198  Add a concept or a rule about it | internal/domain, as a type or method on the type
 199  Add a use case | The owning context's service in internal/application/<ctx>; expose it on a driving port in internal/ports
 200  Talk to a new external system | A driven port in internal/ports, an adapter in internal/adapter/out/<tech>, bound in internal/app
 201  React to something another context did | Subscribe to its domain event in your context's Subscribe(bus); never import its package
 202  Read another context's data | A narrow read port it already satisfies (CapabilityReader, TicketReader)
 203  Expose it over HTTP / MCP | A handler in adapter/in/httpapi or a tool in application/tooling + adapter/in/mcp(session)
 204  Show it in the browser | A templ page in adapter/in/web/<page> and a module in web/src/modules/<module>
 205  Make it work in tui-client too | Implement the driving port in adapter/out/httpclient and run the portstest suite against it
 206  Written from the code on branch main @ 9c1893b, 2026-10-07. Package doc comments in each folder are the source of truth; update this page when a layer or context changes. Design-tab source: design/architecture.html.
